<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siyasatnama</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Siyasat-nama</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siyasatnama, or Book of Government, is a Persian treatise on statecraft written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. It advises rulers on justice, administration and military affairs. Accounts of the Seljuk Empire note its emphasis on professional standing armies and describes it as a foundational text of Islamic political theory.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The work is also known as the Siyar al-muluk, or Lives of Kings, and belongs to the tradition of Persian-Islamic writing known as the Mirrors for Princes. Its author, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, served the Seljuk sultans <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a>, headed the administration of the empire for about 30 years and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyyas</a>, institutions of higher education. He is credited with establishing Persian forms of Islamic government that lasted for centuries.</p><p>The treatise was composed in Persian in the eleventh century after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a> asked his ministers to write books on government, administration and the troubles facing the realm. Of the works produced, only that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> was approved, and the Seljuk court accepted it as the law of the constitution of the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book has 50 chapters on religion, politics and other matters of the day. It sets out the proper role of soldiers, police, spies and finance officials, and gives ethical advice on the ruler's need for justice and religious piety. Justice, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> defines it, means that every class receives its due and the weak are protected; where possible it rests on both custom and Muslim law, and the ruler answers to God.</p><p>The text is full of anecdotes drawn from Islamic and sometimes pre-Islamic Persian history, featuring heroes such as Mahmud of Ghazna and the Shah Khosrow Anushirvan. The final 11 chapters deal mostly with dangers facing the empire, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a>. Tradition holds that they were written shortly before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> was assassinated, but modern scholarship suggests they were heavily interpolated or added later by palace scribes such as Muhammad al-Maghribi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The anecdotes were long read as models of virtue, but modern researchers argue that many were altered to serve as political propaganda and to justify the power of the bureaucracy rather than to record history. The book is valued as evidence of how the Persian bureaucracy saw its civilisation's past and of how far its administration drew on pre-Islamic traditions. The earliest surviving copy is held in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, and the work was first translated into French in 1891.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siyasatnama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nizam al-Mulk's <b>Siyasat-nama</b> set out the principles of military governance, stressing the value of a multi-ethnic professional standing army over reliance on unreliable tribal contingents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
